# Conditional requests

A browser that has seen a page before can ask "has this changed?" instead
of "send me this". When it hasn't, the answer is an empty `304 Not
Modified`, and the browser shows the copy it already has. `howdy/conditional`
answers those questions for your handlers, the same way `howdy/static`
already does for files.

The same headers protect writes. A client that edited an old copy of a
record can be refused with `412 Precondition Failed` instead of overwriting
someone else's change.

## Answering 304

Describe the version of what the handler is about to send, then hand the
rest of the handler to `conditional.when`:

```gleam
import gleam/int
import howdy/conditional
import howdy/controller.{type Context}
import howdy/param
import howdy/service

fn show(ctx: Context) {
  use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")
  case notes.find(db, id) {
    Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
    Ok(note) -> {
      use <- conditional.when(
        ctx,
        conditional.etag(["note", int.to_string(id), int.to_string(note.version)])
          |> conditional.last_modified(note.updated_at),
      )
      controller.json(ctx, note_to_json(note))
    }
  }
}
```

The first response carries an `etag` and a `last-modified` header. When the
browser asks again it sends them back, in `if-none-match` and
`if-modified-since`. While the note is unchanged, `when` answers `304`
straight away: the rest of the handler never runs, the JSON is never built
and no body is sent. Once the note changes, its version changes, so does the
etag, and the handler runs as usual.

The database read still happens, because that is how the handler learns
the version. When that read is the expensive part, keep it in the
[cache](/docs/cache/overview) too.

## Choosing the parts

`conditional.etag` hashes the parts you give it into a short tag. List
everything that changes the response:

- the record's id and a version or update time;
- the signed-in user, for anything that differs per user;
- the language, for a translated page;
- a number you bump by hand when the template changes, if a deploy can
  change the output while the data stays the same.

Getting this wrong only ever sends a `304` too often, so err on the side of
more parts. A missing user id would confirm one user's page to another.

`conditional.modified_at(seconds)` builds validators from a modification
time alone. HTTP dates count whole seconds, so two changes in the same
second look like one; prefer an etag when that matters.

## Who may store the response

`when` sends `cache-control: private, no-cache` unless you say otherwise.
The browser keeps the response but asks before every use, which is what
makes the `304`s happen, and shared caches such as a CDN never store it.

For a response that is the same for everyone, and may be a little stale,
let shared caches keep it:

```gleam
let validators =
  conditional.etag(["pricing", pricing.version])
  |> conditional.cache_control(
    conditional.public()
    |> conditional.max_age(60)
    |> conditional.shared_max_age(600),
  )
```

That sends `public, max-age=60, s-maxage=600`: browsers use their copy for
a minute, a CDN for ten. The `304` carries the same header, so a cache
that asked keeps the policy.

The builder starts from `private`, `public` or `no_store`, and adds
`max_age`, `shared_max_age`, `stale_while_revalidate`, `must_revalidate`
and `immutable`. `conditional.set_cache_control` puts a policy on any
response, and `conditional.without_cache_control` leaves the header to the
handler.

When a response depends on a request header, such as `accept-language`,
name it in `vary` so caches keep one copy per value:

```gleam
controller.html(ctx, page) |> conditional.vary(["accept-language"])
```

## Protecting writes

`when` also checks `if-match` and `if-unmodified-since`, on any method.
Describe the record as it is before the write, and a client that edited an
older copy gets `412 Precondition Failed` before the handler runs.

`if-match` compares only strong tags, so the `GET` the client edits from
has to send a strong one too. Build the validators in one function and use
it in both handlers:

```gleam
fn note_validators(note: Note) -> conditional.Validators {
  conditional.strong_etag(["note", int.to_string(note.id), int.to_string(note.version)])
}

fn show(ctx: Context) {
  use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")
  case notes.find(db, id) {
    Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
    Ok(note) -> {
      use <- conditional.when(ctx, note_validators(note))
      controller.json(ctx, note_to_json(note))
    }
  }
}

fn update(ctx: Context) {
  use id <- param.int(ctx, "id")
  case notes.find(db, id) {
    Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
    Ok(note) -> {
      use <- conditional.when(ctx, note_validators(note))
      // The client's copy was current: go ahead.
      save(ctx, note)
    }
  }
}
```

`when` checks the version before the handler runs, so two `PUT`s that
read the same version can both pass it. To stop the second one
overwriting the first, make the write itself conditional: update only the
version the client saw, and answer `412` when no row matched.

```gleam
// UPDATE app_notes SET body = $1, version = version + 1
// WHERE id = $2 AND version = $3
case notes.update_if_version(db, id, input, expected: note.version) {
  Ok(saved) -> controller.json(ctx, note_to_json(saved))
  Error(service.NotFound(_)) -> controller.status(ctx, 412)
  Error(error) -> service.error_response(ctx, error)
}
```

Locking the row with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` in a transaction around the
read, the check and the write works too.

A strong tag promises the bytes are the same, not just the data, which is
true of a body rendered from the same data by the same code. Leave such
responses uncompressed by middleware that rewrites bodies, or use a weak
`etag` and give up `if-match`. The client reads the tag from `GET`, and
sends it back with its `PUT`:

```sh
curl -X PUT -H 'if-match: "kZ2..."' -d @note.json https://example.com/notes/1
```

Without `if-match` the write goes ahead as before. The response to a write
is not given the old validators, since it describes the new version.

## Without the handler's help

`conditional.auto_etag` is middleware that hashes the body of every `200`
text or JSON response to a `GET` and answers `304` when the client already
has it:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.middleware(conditional.auto_etag)
```

It saves bandwidth, not work: the handler still runs in full each time.
Responses with their own etag, `no-store`, a `set-cookie` header, or a
streamed body are left alone.

## Checking it in tests

Send the validators back with `testing.header`:

```gleam
let first = testing.get("/notes/1") |> testing.send(app())
let assert Ok(etag) = response.get_header(first, "etag")

let again =
  testing.get("/notes/1")
  |> testing.header("if-none-match", etag)
  |> testing.send(app())
assert again.status == 304
```
